.TH BT 1
.SH NAME
bt \- Bluetooth: devices, pairing, connecting, sending files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bt
.RB [ scan " | " list " | " on " | " off " | " accept " | " decline ]
.br
.B bt
.BR pair " | " forget " | " connect " | " disconnect
.I addr
.br
.B bt send
.I addr file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bt
talks to the Bluetooth server,
.BR btd (4),
on its port
.BR bt ;
the desktop's Settings do the same. An
.I addr
is written
.BR AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B bt
The state: the controller, on, off, starting or failed (and the last
failure), its address.
.TP
.B scan
10 s of inquiry and LE scanning, then the list.
.TP
.B list
The devices found and bonded: address, classic or LE, signal, name, kind
(keyboard, mouse, audio, phone, computer), bonded, connected or in use.
.TP
.BI pair " addr"
(root) Pair with a device: shows the code to compare
("Does the device show 123456? [y/n]") or to type on it
("Type 123456 on the device, then Enter."), and waits until it is done.
.TP
.BI forget " addr"
(root) Forget a bonded device.
.TP
.BI connect " addr\fR, " disconnect " addr"
Connect a bonded device (headphones: the sound goes there), or disconnect
it.
.TP
.BI send " addr file"
Send a file to a bonded phone or computer (OBEX Object Push).
.TP
.BR accept ", " decline
(root) Take or refuse the file a device offers (the desktop asks too);
an accepted file goes to the desktop user's
.IR ~/Downloads .
.TP
.BR on ", " off
(root) Turn Bluetooth on or off (kept).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on a failure, a usage error, or with no Bluetooth.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
bt scan
bt pair 00:1A:7D:DA:71:13
bt send 00:1A:7D:DA:71:13 notes.txt
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR btd (4),
.BR audiod (4),
.BR play (1)
.SH BUGS
OBEX: one transfer at a time, Object Push only. A2DP: SBC at 48 kHz only,
one sink at a time. No LE privacy: bonded LE devices that use resolvable
private addresses must be paired again. One adapter.
